  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nA pair of upper-level shortwaves is evident over the Midwest in\nGOES water vapor imagery loops early this morning, with one of \nthese waves drifting eastward across northern Wisconsin and the \nother lifting northeastward into Iowa from the central Plains. \nNear and between those two shortwaves, a thick mid-upper-level \ncloud deck has supported a smattering of 20-40 dbZ radar \nreflectivity returns on regional radar mosaics (including near \nand over our forecast area), though a lot of these returns have \njust been virga with dry low-mid-level air thwarting the \nattempts of descending hydrometeors to reach the ground. While \na modest easterly feed of dry low-mid-level air spurred by \nexpansive high pressure to our northeast will continue to make \nit difficult for a meaningful amount of top-down saturation to \noccur amidst less-than-stellar synoptic-scale forcing for \nascent, the expectation is that enough saturation of the dry \nlow-level air should occur for occasional sprinkles to be \nobserved in our forecast area this morning. Could envision some \nisolated instances of measurable rainfall (no more than a few \nhundredths of an inch) occurring as well, so did continue to \ncarry slight chance PoPs in the forecast grids this morning -- \nprimarily focused over the northern half of our CWA. Slightly \nbelow normal high temperatures in the mid-upper 60s are \notherwise expected today without much additional fanfare. \n\nSimilar to slightly warmer high temperatures are then forecasted\nthrough the weekend and into early next week amidst a mix of \nsunshine and clouds and no real air mass changes of note as a \nRex block develops over the eastern CONUS. Saturday night into \nSunday morning, another disturbance will attempt to make inroads\ninto the Great Lakes from the central Plains. However, its \nnortheastward progress will be halted as it runs into the \naforementioned region of high pressure within the Rex block, and\nsimilar to today, the continuing associated feed of dry low-\nmid- level air will make it difficult for any accompanying \nrainfall to penetrate well into our forecast area. There may \nnevertheless still be a period of decaying showers or sprinkles \nduring this time frame (primarily in our western counties), but \notherwise, dry conditions are favored to prevail over the \nweekend through at least Tuesday morning.\n\nBy mid-week next week, the eastern CONUS Rex block will have \nbroken down, allowing for a more progressive weather pattern to \ntake shape. Exactly how things will play out here mid-late next \nweek remains quite uncertain at this time, largely owing to the \nexpectation that an upper-level trough will likely become cut-\noff from the polar jet stream and then undergo a complex \ninteraction with the remnants of Pacific Hurricane Polo over the\nDesert Southwest midweek. Additional disturbances still \nembedded in the jet stream may then potentially interact with \nwhatever comes of this phasing attempt between these two \nfeatures as they lift northeastward, which further muddles the \npicture here for the end of September and the start of October.\nDespite this, there is fairly strong ensemble support for an \ninflux of moisture and a frontal zone coming through the region \nmid-late next week, so it's a fairly good bet that it will \nprobably rain here at some point (and possibly more than once) \nduring this time frame. Exactly when this rain will occur and \nhow much of it we'll get is something that we won't really be \nable to drill down until forecast trends become clearer in the \ncoming days.\n\nOgorek",
